## THE WORKFLOW — AUDIT FIRST, THEN EDIT, THEN VERIFY (mirror the Cowork two-stage)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Every task runs in three phases. Do not collapse them.

**PHASE 1 — AUDIT (read-only, no edits).**
- Before changing anything, investigate the actual code. Read the real files, schema,
  types, exact literals (column names, enums, IDs, CSV headers, provenance labels).
- Ground every literal against the ACTUAL current file — cite file:line. Do NOT trust
  your codebase index, memory, or assumption; open and read the real file.
- Report what you found and what you propose to change, scoped to the prompt.
- DO NOT EDIT in this phase. Show the audit first.

**PHASE 2 — EDIT (only the named scope).**
- Implement exactly the scoped change, grounded in what Phase 1 confirmed.
- Touch only the files named. Smallest diff. Preserve everything else.
- State clearly what is real vs. placeholder if the task involves either.

**PHASE 3 — VERIFY (before calling it done).**
- Independently check the change against the prompt's acceptance criteria.
- State honestly what you verified and what you did NOT / could not verify.
- A passing build, a clean compile, or "it looks right" is NOT verification of behavior.
  Flag anything that needs live confirmation (UI, auth-gated flows, DB changes) so Ryan
  can confirm on the real running app. Never claim "done/verified" for something only
  compiled, not actually run.

